## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical E2E skills: gating UX with feature flags, deterministic tests, data seeding, and test isolation using Cypress or Playwright.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gating
- Test data seeding per run
- LocalStorage state as idempotence marker
- Network/mocks to deterministically simulate flag responses
- Time-bounded execution for beginner scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton
test('onboarding respects flag', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/flags**', r => r.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ onboarding: true }) }));
  await page.goto('https://app.local/');
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeVisible();
  await page.locator('#finish').click();
  const stored = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('onboarding_complete'));
  expect(stored).toBe('true');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to multi-tenant setups with per-tenant flag overrides?
- How would you ensure the test remains reliable when the flag toggles during a session?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests basic E2E skills on a realistic, client-side onboarding flow, including localStorage persistence, keyboard accessibility, and deterministic API mocking for repeatable results.

## Key Concepts

- E2E test isolation and deterministic mocks
- LocalStorage state persistence across refresh
- Keyboard navigation and accessibility
- Test data seeding and cleanup
- Cross-browser considerations for beginner level

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Cypress test
describe('Onboarding wizard', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.clearLocalStorage();
    cy.viewport(1024, 768);
  });

  it('persists progress and uses deterministic API', () => {
    cy.intercept('POST', '/api/create-account', { id: 'user-123', status: 'ok' }).as('create');
    cy.visit('/onboarding');
    cy.get('[data-testid="step1-name"]').type('Alex');
    cy.get('[data-testid="step1-name"]').should('have.value', 'Alex');
    cy.get('[data-testid="next"]').click();
    cy.get('[data-testid="step2-email"]').type('alex@example.com');
    cy.get('[data-testid="submit"]').click();
    cy.wait('@create');
    cy.get('[data-testid="final-title"]').should('contain', 'Welcome, Alex');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to verify persistence across browser restarts in multiple browsers?
- How would you test accessibility and keyboard-only navigation for all steps?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's understanding of end-to-end testing for multi-step user journeys, including authentication flows, external system mocking, and deterministic test data management. It evaluates practical knowledge of creating reliable, fast, and isolated tests in a real-world SaaS context.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing with authentication and email verification
- Test data seeding and database isolation
- Mocking external services (email delivery, OIDC sign-in)
- Assertions for UI state and created resources
- Time-bounded test design for CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Using Playwright
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('complete onboarding flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed deterministic test data
  await seedTestUser({ email: 'demo@test.com', password: 'Test123!' });

  // Mock email service to capture verification link
  await mockEmailService();

  // Mock OIDC provider
  await mockOIDCProvider();

  // Navigate to signup and complete registration
  await page.goto('/signup');
  await page.fill('[name=email]', 'demo@test.com');
  await page.fill('[name=password]', 'Test123!');
  await page.click('[data-testid=signup-submit]');

  // Extract and follow email verification link
  const verifyLink = await getVerificationLink('demo@test.com');
  await page.goto(verifyLink);

  // Complete profile setup
  await page.fill('[name=name]', 'Jane Doe');
  await page.fill('[name=company]', 'Acme Corp');
  await page.click('[data-testid=profile-submit]');

  // Create first workspace
  await page.fill('[name=workspaceName]', 'My Workspace');
  await page.click('[data-testid=create-workspace]');

  // Assert dashboard displays new workspace
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=workspace-card]')).toContainText('My Workspace');
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests involving background work and eventual UI changes are common in data apps. This question checks understanding of test isolation, idempotency, and end-to-end timing without flakiness.

## Key Concepts

- Background job queues and completion signaling
- API and UI mocking for determinism
- Data seeding and range validation
- Test isolation and deterministic timing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright pseudo-example
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('export enqueues, completes, and provides download', async ({ page }) => {
  await seedDataForRange('2023-01-01','2023-01-31');
  await page.goto('/export');
  await page.fill('#start', '2023-01-01');
  await page.fill('#end', '2023-01-31');
  await page.click('#submit-export');
  await expect(page.locator('#progress')).toHaveText(/in progress/);
  await mockDelay(10000); // simulate 10s processing
  await page.waitForSelector('#download-link', { timeout: 20000 });
  const csv = await page.locator('#download-link').evaluate(node => fetch(node.href).then(r => r.text()));
  expect(csv).toContain('2023-01-01,2023-01-31');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test to handle export failures and retry logic?
- What patterns help keep tests fast while still validating end-to-end behavior?

